 ## Overview
-Primitive Geometry is the foundational layer of the Splatting family that defines how volumetric data is decomposed into explicit geometric or parametric primitives for rasterization [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping 3D volumetric information to 2D screen space by treating voxels or data elements as shapes (e.g., spheres, quads, capsules) that are transformed and blended [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It differs from ray-casting methods by computing contributions per primitive rather than per pixel, and from sibling methods by providing the base definitions for primitive types, projection, and culling that specific implementations like [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) or [Circle Splat Rendering](2981-circle-splat-rendering.md) refine.
+Primitive Geometry is the foundational family of splatting methods that decompose volumetric data into explicit geometric or parametric primitives (such as voxels, tetrahedra, or line segments) which are transformed and rasterized in screen space [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It differs from ray-casting by computing contributions per primitive rather than per pixel, leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization for real-time performance [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
