 ## Background
-The concept of primitive geometry in volume rendering originates from the shift from ray-based to object-order rendering techniques. Early work by Levoy [S3] and Westover [S4] established the theoretical basis for splatting, treating voxels as 3D kernels projected onto the screen. The family has evolved to include specialized primitives for specific data types, such as lines [S1] and tetrahedra [S1], and has been adapted for modern hardware acceleration [S1, S2, S3]. Recent developments include Resonant Brane Splatting for super-resolution [S1] and triangle mesh approximations for rasterization efficiency [S1].
